    scene: "A clear front-facing portrait of an ordinary adult in soft daylight, no sunglasses, no beauty filter, no text",
    cta: "Lock one reference, then vary the room",
    answer: "A reference photo for one face is a clear picture of that person, not a mood. Front or three-quarter, soft window light from one side, eyes visible and sharp, no sunglasses, no heavy filter, no second person in the crop. Crop above the hair and at the collar. The near cheek is brighter. The far cheek drops. That frame is the identity. Later stills may change the room and the shirt. They may not change the eyes, the nose, or the age. If the reference is already filtered, every picture after it will inherit the plastic, and that plastic is visible on a phone as a smooth cheek you then lock forever. Generate new stills from this reference, compare, reject the drift, and regenerate. A realistic face still needs an AI label. A cartoon is a different case.",
    points: [
      { h: "One person in the crop", p: "A group photo makes the model guess who you meant, and on a phone you can see a second shoulder or a second eye at the edge. Crop to the face you are locking. Hands near the face confuse the edges, so crop them out or keep them down on the table. A plain background is a gift, not a requirement. The window can stay in frame as the light. Generate from that single-person reference, compare the count of faces, reject any still that invented a companion, and regenerate. Eyes stay sharp. One catchlight, one direction." },
      { h: "Light you can repeat", p: "A reference shot in hard neon is a hard identity to keep on a Tuesday morning. Prefer the window light you actually want in the feed: one side brighter, far cheek darker, eyes sharp. You can change rooms later. You should not have to undo a filter. On a phone, a neon reference next to a daylight still looks like two shoots of two people. Generate the Tuesday still from the window reference, compare the cheek falloff, reject a new light that redraws the nose, and regenerate in the same window logic. Crop the wild background. Keep the face large." },
      { h: "Compare, do not vibe", p: "Put the new still beside the reference on the phone. Eyes, nose, jaw, hairline. \"Close enough\" is how an account becomes two people. Reject the still that drifted, even if the new room is prettier, and regenerate from the same reference with those four points named. Do not crop the hairline out, or you cannot compare it. Do not accept a softer ear as the same head. The shirt may change. The light may move to a new window on the same side. The identity stays sharp and comparable." },
    ],
    example: "Reference, which is also Monday: one front portrait by a window, camera-left light, no glasses glare, no second face, eyes and hairline sharp, crop at the collar. Generate Thursday's cafe still from that photo, same face, new room, same light logic. Compare on a phone. Accept Thursday only because the eyes, nose, jaw, and hairline match Monday. Reject a prettier cafe frame where the jaw narrowed. Regenerate Thursday from Monday's reference.",
    bridge: "Scro locks that identity so wardrobe and Life photos stay the same person. Approve each new still against the reference before you render a clip or post the photo. If it drifted, reject it and regenerate. The voice you clone should belong to this same host. Export 9:16. Label the realistic face as AI. A cartoon is a different case.",
    checks: ["The reference shows one clear face.", "Eyes are not hidden or filtered.", "New stills were compared to it."],
